1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,680
In February of 1982,

2
00:00:01,740 --> 00:00:03,839
a doctor at the Cook County Jail examined

3
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:04,539
a new prisoner

4
00:00:04,540 --> 00:00:07,256
and found injuries he could not explain away.

5
00:00:07,316 --> 00:00:09,115
Burns on the man's chest and thigh,

6
00:00:09,116 --> 00:00:10,875
seared in neat lines,

7
00:00:10,876 --> 00:00:13,547
as if he'd been held against something hot.

8
00:00:13,607 --> 00:00:16,387
Small scabbed wounds on his ears, his nose,

9
00:00:16,447 --> 00:00:17,707
his fingers —

10
00:00:17,767 --> 00:00:20,767
marks the doctor had no polite medical name for,

11
00:00:20,827 --> 00:00:23,473
because they were the marks of electrical clips.

12
00:00:23,474 --> 00:00:26,686
So the doctor did what doctors are supposed to do.

13
00:00:26,746 --> 00:00:28,345
He wrote a letter to the superintendent

14
00:00:28,346 --> 00:00:30,326
of the Chicago Police Department,

15
00:00:30,386 --> 00:00:32,504
describing the injuries, demanding —

16
00:00:32,546 --> 00:00:33,526
his words —

17
00:00:33,586 --> 00:00:35,485
a thorough investigation.

18
00:00:35,545 --> 00:00:37,804
That letter went up the chain of command,

19
00:00:37,805 --> 00:00:38,884
landed on the desk of one

20
00:00:38,885 --> 00:00:41,065
of the most powerful men in Illinois,

21
00:00:41,125 --> 00:00:42,519
and died there.

22
00:00:42,579 --> 00:00:43,748
No answer.

23
00:00:43,808 --> 00:00:45,302
No investigation.

24
00:00:45,362 --> 00:00:46,438
Nothing.

25
00:00:46,498 --> 00:00:48,397
It would take Chicago thirty-three years

26
00:00:48,398 --> 00:00:50,517
to say out loud what that one page said

27
00:00:50,518 --> 00:00:51,858
in 1982 —

28
00:00:51,918 --> 00:00:53,938
and when the city finally did,

29
00:00:53,998 --> 00:00:57,665
it would say it in a schoolbook.

30
00:00:57,725 --> 00:00:59,625
The man the letter was about —

31
00:00:59,685 --> 00:01:01,444
the man whose detectives put those marks

32
00:01:01,445 --> 00:01:02,725
on that prisoner —

33
00:01:02,785 --> 00:01:04,559
was named Jon Burge.

34
00:01:04,619 --> 00:01:07,059
He was a decorated Vietnam veteran,

35
00:01:07,119 --> 00:01:09,899
a rising star of the Chicago Police Department,

36
00:01:09,959 --> 00:01:11,358
and for almost twenty years —

37
00:01:11,359 --> 00:01:13,439
as detective, sergeant,

38
00:01:13,499 --> 00:01:16,339
and finally commander on the city's South Side —

39
00:01:16,399 --> 00:01:18,498
he and the men around him tortured confessions

40
00:01:18,499 --> 00:01:21,252
out of more than one hundred human beings.

41
00:01:21,312 --> 00:01:22,945
Electric shock.

42
00:01:23,005 --> 00:01:24,360
Suffocation.

43
00:01:24,420 --> 00:01:26,378
Mock executions.

44
00:01:26,438 --> 00:01:27,817
More than a hundred victims —

45
00:01:27,818 --> 00:01:31,043
and almost every one of them a Black man.

46
00:01:31,103 --> 00:01:33,322
Now hold on to the fact this entire film hangs

47
00:01:33,323 --> 00:01:34,223
on:

48
00:01:34,283 --> 00:01:37,009
Jon Burge was never charged with torture.

49
00:01:37,069 --> 00:01:38,795
Not in 1982.

50
00:01:38,855 --> 00:01:40,813
Not in 1993.

51
00:01:40,873 --> 00:01:42,088
Not ever.

52
00:01:42,148 --> 00:01:44,567
And yet he died a convicted felon who served time

53
00:01:44,568 --> 00:01:46,010
in a federal prison.

54
00:01:46,070 --> 00:01:48,229
Both of those things are true at once,

55
00:01:48,230 --> 00:01:50,090
and the space between them —

56
00:01:50,150 --> 00:01:51,569
how the law finally reached

57
00:01:51,570 --> 00:01:54,050
a man it had already forgiven —

58
00:01:54,110 --> 00:01:56,709
is the strangest, and maybe the most honest,

59
00:01:56,710 --> 00:02:00,242
story American justice has ever told about itself.

60
00:02:00,302 --> 00:02:01,749
Remember the letter.

61
00:02:01,809 --> 00:02:04,803
It's coming back.

62
00:02:04,863 --> 00:02:05,882
To understand how

63
00:02:05,883 --> 00:02:07,602
a torture chamber operated inside

64
00:02:07,603 --> 00:02:10,343
an American police station for two decades,

65
00:02:10,403 --> 00:02:12,342
you have to understand that nobody built it

66
00:02:12,343 --> 00:02:13,462
in secret.

67
00:02:13,522 --> 00:02:14,801
It was built in plain sight,

68
00:02:14,802 --> 00:02:17,013
by a man the city kept promoting.

69
00:02:17,073 --> 00:02:19,513
Jon Burge grew up in South Deering,

70
00:02:19,573 --> 00:02:22,773
a steel-mill neighborhood
on Chicago's Southeast Side —

71
00:02:22,833 --> 00:02:24,572
a white enclave in a part of the city

72
00:02:24,573 --> 00:02:26,229
that was changing around it.

73
00:02:26,289 --> 00:02:28,247
At eighteen he joined the Army.

74
00:02:28,307 --> 00:02:30,227
He volunteered for Vietnam,

75
00:02:30,287 --> 00:02:31,586
served as a military policeman

76
00:02:31,587 --> 00:02:33,747
with the Ninth Infantry Division,

77
00:02:33,807 --> 00:02:37,207
and came home in 1969 with a Bronze Star,

78
00:02:37,267 --> 00:02:40,900
a Purple Heart, and a chest full of commendations.

79
00:02:40,960 --> 00:02:44,265
A war hero, by every measure his country used.

80
00:02:44,325 --> 00:02:46,124
In March of 1970 he joined

81
00:02:46,125 --> 00:02:47,890
the Chicago Police Department,

82
00:02:47,905 --> 00:02:50,465
and his rise was close to vertical:

83
00:02:50,525 --> 00:02:54,125
detective within two years, sergeant by 1977,

84
00:02:54,185 --> 00:02:55,965
lieutenant by 1981 —

85
00:02:56,025 --> 00:02:58,724
and that same year, still in his early thirties,

86
00:02:58,725 --> 00:02:59,564
he was given command

87
00:02:59,565 --> 00:03:02,625
of the brand-new Violent Crimes unit at Area 2,

88
00:03:02,685 --> 00:03:04,964
the detective division covering a huge stretch

89
00:03:04,965 --> 00:03:06,392
of the South Side.

90
00:03:06,452 --> 00:03:08,828
His numbers were spectacular.

91
00:03:08,888 --> 00:03:10,289
Cases closed.

92
00:03:10,349 --> 00:03:11,889
Confessions signed.

93
00:03:11,949 --> 00:03:13,443
Convictions won.

94
00:03:13,503 --> 00:03:15,923
The department gave him commendations;

95
00:03:15,983 --> 00:03:18,062
the newspapers gave him headlines.

96
00:03:18,122 --> 00:03:19,901
Nobody in charge asked the one question

97
00:03:19,902 --> 00:03:21,142
that mattered:

98
00:03:21,202 --> 00:03:23,801
why did so many suspects, in this one building,

99
00:03:23,802 --> 00:03:25,601
under this one commander,

100
00:03:25,602 --> 00:03:27,882
sign so many confessions?

101
00:03:27,942 --> 00:03:29,343
Here is why.

102
00:03:29,403 --> 00:03:31,483
On the midnight shift at Area 2,

103
00:03:31,543 --> 00:03:34,123
a group of detectives worked under Burge —

104
00:03:34,183 --> 00:03:37,123
white men, working a Black part of the city,

105
00:03:37,183 --> 00:03:39,142
who would later become known by a nickname

106
00:03:39,143 --> 00:03:40,863
that sounds like pulp fiction

107
00:03:40,923 --> 00:03:43,423
and is instead sworn court record:

108
00:03:43,483 --> 00:03:44,968
the Midnight Crew.

109
00:03:45,028 --> 00:03:47,172
And the Midnight Crew had tools.

110
00:03:47,232 --> 00:03:48,272
There was a box —

111
00:03:48,332 --> 00:03:50,932
a plain, dark, hand-cranked device,

112
00:03:50,992 --> 00:03:52,291
the size of a lunch pail,

113
00:03:52,292 --> 00:03:54,392
with two wires coming out of it.

114
00:03:54,452 --> 00:03:56,611
Men who survived that room describe it

115
00:03:56,612 --> 00:03:58,731
the same way, across decades,

116
00:03:58,732 --> 00:04:01,192
without ever having met each other:

117
00:04:01,252 --> 00:04:02,131
the crank turns,

118
00:04:02,132 --> 00:04:06,052
and electricity goes into your ears, your fingers,

119
00:04:06,112 --> 00:04:08,211
your genitals, until you say what the men

120
00:04:08,212 --> 00:04:10,017
in the room want you to say.

121
00:04:10,077 --> 00:04:12,336
Detectives kept plastic typewriter covers

122
00:04:12,337 --> 00:04:13,757
in the office —

123
00:04:13,817 --> 00:04:16,117
ordinary gray office plastic —

124
00:04:16,177 --> 00:04:18,456
and they had a use for them that had nothing

125
00:04:18,457 --> 00:04:19,837
to do with typewriters:

126
00:04:19,897 --> 00:04:22,056
over a man's head, held tight,

127
00:04:22,057 --> 00:04:24,249
until the world went dark.

128
00:04:24,309 --> 00:04:25,989
They called it bagging.

129
00:04:26,049 --> 00:04:27,988
There were guns pressed to heads in games

130
00:04:27,989 --> 00:04:30,515
the suspect didn't know were games.

131
00:04:30,575 --> 00:04:32,435
And there was the radiator —

132
00:04:32,495 --> 00:04:34,155
cast iron, hissing hot —

133
00:04:34,215 --> 00:04:35,754
for handcuffed men to be held

134
00:04:35,755 --> 00:04:37,174
against until the burns rose

135
00:04:37,175 --> 00:04:39,128
in neat parallel lines.

136
00:04:39,188 --> 00:04:41,907
I am telling you this in a list, clinically, once,

137
00:04:41,908 --> 00:04:43,527
because you need to know exactly what

138
00:04:43,528 --> 00:04:45,837
the word "torture" means in this story.

139
00:04:45,897 --> 00:04:47,902
The film will not show it to you.

140
00:04:47,962 --> 00:04:49,502
The record is enough.

141
00:04:49,562 --> 00:04:51,381
Where did the box come from?

142
00:04:51,441 --> 00:04:52,200
Men who served

143
00:04:52,201 --> 00:04:54,200
in Burge's own military police company

144
00:04:54,201 --> 00:04:56,980
in Vietnam later told reporters they had seen

145
00:04:56,981 --> 00:04:58,480
prisoners interrogated there

146
00:04:58,481 --> 00:05:01,541
with hand-cranked field telephones —

147
00:05:01,601 --> 00:05:04,127
wires, crank, current.

148
00:05:04,187 --> 00:05:07,266
Burge denied ever seeing or doing any such thing,

149
00:05:07,267 --> 00:05:09,396
in Vietnam or in Chicago.

150
00:05:09,456 --> 00:05:11,755
What nobody disputes is that within a few years

151
00:05:11,756 --> 00:05:13,336
of his coming home,

152
00:05:13,396 --> 00:05:15,615
a hand-cranked electrical device was being turned

153
00:05:15,616 --> 00:05:18,035
on handcuffed men in a police station

154
00:05:18,036 --> 00:05:20,191
on the South Side of Chicago.

155
00:05:20,251 --> 00:05:21,698
It started early.

156
00:05:21,758 --> 00:05:23,578
In May of 1973,

157
00:05:23,638 --> 00:05:26,422
detectives brought in a man named Anthony Holmes.

158
00:05:26,423 --> 00:05:29,763
Decades later, under oath in a federal courtroom,

159
00:05:29,823 --> 00:05:32,242
Holmes would describe what Burge did to him:

160
00:05:32,243 --> 00:05:34,803
"He plugged it into the wall and came towards me.

161
00:05:34,804 --> 00:05:35,323
He said,

162
00:05:35,324 --> 00:05:37,523
'You're going to tell us what we want to know.'"

163
00:05:37,524 --> 00:05:38,564
And then:

164
00:05:38,624 --> 00:05:41,280
"I didn't know if it was daytime or nighttime.

165
00:05:41,281 --> 00:05:42,914
He just kept shocking me.

166
00:05:42,974 --> 00:05:45,257
Holmes blacked out, again and again.

167
00:05:45,317 --> 00:05:47,461
When he could speak, he confessed.

168
00:05:47,521 --> 00:05:49,741
The confession became a conviction,

169
00:05:49,801 --> 00:05:52,061
the conviction became thirty years,

170
00:05:52,121 --> 00:05:54,140
and the thirty years became a commendation

171
00:05:54,141 --> 00:05:56,074
in somebody's personnel file.

172
00:05:56,134 --> 00:05:58,033
And that is the machine, whole and running,

173
00:05:58,034 --> 00:05:59,625
in one paragraph.

174
00:05:59,685 --> 00:06:00,645
A suspect —

175
00:06:00,705 --> 00:06:04,205
almost always a Black man, often with a record,

176
00:06:04,265 --> 00:06:07,544
the kind of man 1970s Chicago had decided

177
00:06:07,545 --> 00:06:09,765
in advance not to believe —

178
00:06:09,825 --> 00:06:11,489
goes into a room.

179
00:06:11,549 --> 00:06:13,507
Pain comes out of a box.

180
00:06:13,567 --> 00:06:15,804
A confession comes out of the pain.

181
00:06:15,864 --> 00:06:18,287
A conviction comes out of the confession.

182
00:06:18,347 --> 00:06:19,966
And the commander's career comes

183
00:06:19,967 --> 00:06:21,652
out of the convictions.

184
00:06:21,712 --> 00:06:24,371
Every man who stumbled out of Area 2 and told

185
00:06:24,372 --> 00:06:26,831
a public defender what had happened to him ran

186
00:06:26,832 --> 00:06:28,392
into the same wall:

187
00:06:28,452 --> 00:06:29,852
it was his word —

188
00:06:29,912 --> 00:06:31,892
a word already labeled criminal —

189
00:06:31,952 --> 00:06:34,351
against the word of a decorated war hero

190
00:06:34,352 --> 00:06:35,745
with a badge.

191
00:06:35,805 --> 00:06:37,584
The tally climbed through the seventies

192
00:06:37,585 --> 00:06:39,110
and into the eighties.

193
00:06:39,170 --> 00:06:40,385
One man.

194
00:06:40,445 --> 00:06:41,474
Five.

195
00:06:41,534 --> 00:06:42,749
Twenty.

196
00:06:42,809 --> 00:06:44,068
The number will keep climbing

197
00:06:44,069 --> 00:06:45,408
for this entire film,

198
00:06:45,409 --> 00:06:47,468
and I want you to watch it climb,

199
00:06:47,469 --> 00:06:50,048
because the click of that counter is the sound

200
00:06:50,049 --> 00:06:52,709
of a city deciding, over and over,

201
00:06:52,769 --> 00:06:55,170
year after year, not to listen.

202
00:06:55,230 --> 00:06:57,070
Then, in February of 1982,

203
00:06:57,130 --> 00:06:59,789
the machine made its one great mistake.

204
00:06:59,849 --> 00:07:00,988
It left marks on a man

205
00:07:00,989 --> 00:07:04,375
the whole world was watching.

206
00:07:04,435 --> 00:07:06,515
On February 9th, 1982,

207
00:07:06,575 --> 00:07:08,435
two Chicago police officers,

208
00:07:08,495 --> 00:07:10,975
William Fahey and Richard O'Brien,

209
00:07:11,035 --> 00:07:13,638
were shot and killed during a traffic stop.

210
00:07:13,698 --> 00:07:15,857
The murder of two officers set off one

211
00:07:15,858 --> 00:07:18,638
of the largest manhunts in the city's history —

212
00:07:18,698 --> 00:07:21,058
days of raids across the South Side,

213
00:07:21,118 --> 00:07:24,526
doors kicked in, a neighborhood turned inside out.

214
00:07:24,586 --> 00:07:27,245
On February 14th, they caught the man who did it:

215
00:07:27,246 --> 00:07:28,448
Andrew Wilson.

216
00:07:28,508 --> 00:07:30,787
Let me be straight with you about Andrew Wilson,

217
00:07:30,788 --> 00:07:33,485
because this story only works if I am.

218
00:07:33,545 --> 00:07:34,853
He was guilty.

219
00:07:34,913 --> 00:07:36,652
He murdered those two officers,

220
00:07:36,653 --> 00:07:39,952
a jury said so twice, and this film mourns Fahey

221
00:07:39,953 --> 00:07:42,026
and O'Brien without reservation.

222
00:07:42,086 --> 00:07:43,845
Andrew Wilson is not in this story

223
00:07:43,846 --> 00:07:45,391
because he was innocent.

224
00:07:45,451 --> 00:07:46,490
He is in this story

225
00:07:46,491 --> 00:07:49,170
because of what fourteen hours inside Area 2

226
00:07:49,171 --> 00:07:50,391
did to him —

227
00:07:50,451 --> 00:07:51,850
and because the American rule

228
00:07:51,851 --> 00:07:54,490
against torturing prisoners is not a prize

229
00:07:54,491 --> 00:07:55,815
for the innocent.

230
00:07:55,875 --> 00:07:56,955
It is a line,

231
00:07:57,015 --> 00:07:58,854
and it either holds for the worst man

232
00:07:58,855 --> 00:08:01,223
in the room or it holds for no one.

233
00:08:01,283 --> 00:08:03,262
Wilson came into Area 2 in the custody

234
00:08:03,263 --> 00:08:05,462
of Jon Burge's detectives on the morning

235
00:08:05,463 --> 00:08:06,817
of the 14th.

236
00:08:06,877 --> 00:08:08,756
When he was delivered to the county jail,

237
00:08:08,757 --> 00:08:11,016
the intake staff counted roughly fifteen separate

238
00:08:11,017 --> 00:08:12,086
injuries.

239
00:08:12,146 --> 00:08:14,566
Cuts and bruises from beatings, yes —

240
00:08:14,626 --> 00:08:16,426
but also the other marks.

241
00:08:16,486 --> 00:08:18,909
Scabbed clip-wounds on his ears and nose.

242
00:08:18,969 --> 00:08:21,069
And on his chest and thigh,

243
00:08:21,129 --> 00:08:23,128
second-degree burns laid out in straight,

244
00:08:23,129 --> 00:08:24,749
parallel lines —

245
00:08:24,809 --> 00:08:26,508
lines that matched, exactly,

246
00:08:26,509 --> 00:08:28,448
the ribs of the steam radiator

247
00:08:28,449 --> 00:08:30,076
in the interview room.

248
00:08:30,136 --> 00:08:31,975
He had been shocked with the box,

249
00:08:31,976 --> 00:08:33,235
burned against the iron,

250
00:08:33,236 --> 00:08:34,935
and a gun had been put in his mouth,

251
00:08:34,936 --> 00:08:36,599
until he confessed.

252
00:08:36,659 --> 00:08:37,818
Three days later comes

253
00:08:37,819 --> 00:08:39,639
the letter you already know.

254
00:08:39,699 --> 00:08:42,198
Dr. John Raba, medical director at the jail,

255
00:08:42,199 --> 00:08:45,079
examined Wilson, wrote down what he found,

256
00:08:45,139 --> 00:08:45,738
and sent it

257
00:08:45,739 --> 00:08:47,858
to Police Superintendent Richard Brzeczek

258
00:08:47,859 --> 00:08:50,418
demanding "a thorough investigation

259
00:08:50,419 --> 00:08:52,478
of this alleged brutality.

260
00:08:52,538 --> 00:08:54,878
Brzeczek did not simply shred it —

261
00:08:54,938 --> 00:08:57,858
what he did was, in its way, worse,

262
00:08:57,918 --> 00:08:59,744
because it was procedure.

263
00:08:59,804 --> 00:09:00,723
He forwarded the letter

264
00:09:00,724 --> 00:09:02,823
to the Cook County State's Attorney,

265
00:09:02,824 --> 00:09:04,623
the chief prosecutor of the county,

266
00:09:04,624 --> 00:09:06,443
and asked how he should proceed,

267
00:09:06,444 --> 00:09:07,803
since prosecuting Wilson

268
00:09:07,804 --> 00:09:10,911
and investigating his torturers might collide.

269
00:09:10,971 --> 00:09:13,592
The State's Attorney's office never answered him.

270
00:09:13,593 --> 00:09:16,693
Not a letter, not a phone call, nothing —

271
00:09:16,753 --> 00:09:19,272
while that same office took Wilson's tortured

272
00:09:19,273 --> 00:09:22,471
confession and used it to win a death sentence.

273
00:09:22,531 --> 00:09:24,250
The prosecutor who received that letter

274
00:09:24,251 --> 00:09:26,765
and let it die was named Richard M. Daley.

275
00:09:26,825 --> 00:09:28,985
Hold that name however you like;

276
00:09:29,045 --> 00:09:30,804
he would go on to be mayor of Chicago

277
00:09:30,805 --> 00:09:33,105
for twenty-two years.

278
00:09:33,165 --> 00:09:35,544
A quarter of a century later,

279
00:09:35,545 --> 00:09:37,604
official investigators would put it in writing:

280
00:09:37,605 --> 00:09:39,524
the letter reached his desk,

281
00:09:39,525 --> 00:09:41,170
and no investigation of any kind followed.

282
00:09:41,171 --> 00:09:42,851
The letter was buried.

283
00:09:42,911 --> 00:09:45,676
But paper has a strange property in this story:

284
00:09:45,711 --> 00:09:47,284
it keeps coming back.

285
00:09:47,344 --> 00:09:48,843
It came back in 1987,

286
00:09:48,844 --> 00:09:51,563
when the Illinois Supreme Court threw out Wilson's

287
00:09:51,564 --> 00:09:54,143
conviction because the confession had been beaten

288
00:09:54,144 --> 00:09:55,344
out of him —

289
00:09:55,404 --> 00:09:58,172
the court could count radiator ribs too.

290
00:09:58,232 --> 00:10:00,451
He was retried without the confession,

291
00:10:00,452 --> 00:10:02,612
convicted again on other evidence,

292
00:10:02,672 --> 00:10:04,911
and sent away for life, which tells you

293
00:10:04,912 --> 00:10:07,342
the torture was never even necessary.

294
00:10:07,402 --> 00:10:11,122
And it came back in 1989, when Wilson —

295
00:10:11,182 --> 00:10:14,162
a cop-killer with nothing left to lose,

296
00:10:14,222 --> 00:10:15,441
represented by a small band

297
00:10:15,442 --> 00:10:16,501
of civil-rights lawyers

298
00:10:16,502 --> 00:10:18,602
at the People's Law Office —

299
00:10:18,662 --> 00:10:20,722
sued Jon Burge in federal court,

300
00:10:20,782 --> 00:10:22,322
and for the first time,

301
00:10:22,382 --> 00:10:24,641
the box itself was carried into a courtroom

302
00:10:24,642 --> 00:10:26,264
for jurors to see.

303
00:10:26,324 --> 00:10:27,723
The city's lawyers fought the case

304
00:10:27,724 --> 00:10:30,419
with everything, and the first jury hung.

305
00:10:30,479 --> 00:10:31,578
But during that trial,

306
00:10:31,579 --> 00:10:33,358
something extraordinary happened:

307
00:10:33,359 --> 00:10:34,818
anonymous letters began arriving

308
00:10:34,819 --> 00:10:36,338
at the lawyers' office.

309
00:10:36,398 --> 00:10:38,077
Four of them, over five months,

310
00:10:38,078 --> 00:10:40,518
written by someone inside Area 2 —

311
00:10:40,578 --> 00:10:43,976
a police source the lawyers code-named Deep Badge.

312
00:10:44,036 --> 00:10:45,475
The letters said, in effect:

313
00:10:45,476 --> 00:10:47,713
you have no idea how big this is.

314
00:10:47,773 --> 00:10:49,638
They pointed to other victims.

315
00:10:49,698 --> 00:10:51,657
They pointed to a man named Melvin Jones,

316
00:10:51,658 --> 00:10:53,537
who had been shocked with the box

317
00:10:53,538 --> 00:10:55,357
by Burge himself just nine days

318
00:10:55,358 --> 00:10:57,058
before Andrew Wilson —

319
00:10:57,118 --> 00:11:00,318
same device, same hands,

320
00:11:00,378 --> 00:11:01,917
different man with no connection

321
00:11:01,918 --> 00:11:03,777
to any dead officer.

322
00:11:03,837 --> 00:11:05,137
The pattern was there,

323
00:11:05,197 --> 00:11:07,197
in the department's own knowledge,

324
00:11:07,217 --> 00:11:09,876
mailed out of the building by one of its own —

325
00:11:09,877 --> 00:11:11,936
and still the second jury returned a verdict

326
00:11:11,937 --> 00:11:13,496
so tangled it found

327
00:11:13,497 --> 00:11:15,356
the city had a de facto policy

328
00:11:15,357 --> 00:11:17,696
of abusing cop-killing suspects

329
00:11:17,697 --> 00:11:20,424
while somehow clearing the men who did it.

330
00:11:20,484 --> 00:11:23,063
It took a federal appeals court, in 1993,

331
00:11:23,064 --> 00:11:25,600
to sweep that nonsense aside.

332
00:11:25,660 --> 00:11:26,299
Meanwhile,

333
00:11:26,300 --> 00:11:28,653
a reporter had been sitting in that courtroom.

334
00:11:28,654 --> 00:11:30,434
His name was John Conroy,

335
00:11:30,494 --> 00:11:33,133
and in January of 1990 he published a story

336
00:11:33,134 --> 00:11:34,714
in the Chicago Reader —

337
00:11:34,774 --> 00:11:36,094
a long, meticulous,

338
00:11:36,154 --> 00:11:38,971
devastating account called "House of Screams.

339
00:11:39,031 --> 00:11:40,678
It laid the whole thing out:

340
00:11:40,731 --> 00:11:43,636
the box, the burns, the letter, the pattern.

341
00:11:43,696 --> 00:11:47,715
And here is the part of this story
that should frighten you more than the box does:

342
00:11:47,716 --> 00:11:49,462
almost nothing happened.

343
00:11:49,522 --> 00:11:52,688
No wave of prosecutions, no citywide reckoning.

344
00:11:52,748 --> 00:11:54,527
Conroy would write twenty-three articles

345
00:11:54,528 --> 00:11:57,288
about Area 2 over seventeen years —

346
00:11:57,348 --> 00:11:59,107
a man standing in the town square pointing

347
00:11:59,108 --> 00:12:02,468
at a fire, in print, with footnotes —

348
00:12:02,528 --> 00:12:03,847
and for most of those years

349
00:12:03,848 --> 00:12:06,456
the city simply declined to turn its head.

350
00:12:06,516 --> 00:12:08,289
The screams had been heard.

351
00:12:08,349 --> 00:12:10,354
Hearing them changed nothing.

352
00:12:10,414 --> 00:12:12,994
That is what impunity actually looks like:

353
00:12:13,054 --> 00:12:14,174
not a secret,

354
00:12:14,234 --> 00:12:17,016
but a fact everyone has agreed to live with.

355
00:12:17,076 --> 00:12:18,849
The paper kept coming.

356
00:12:18,909 --> 00:12:19,789
In 1990,

357
00:12:19,849 --> 00:12:22,648
the police department's own internal investigator,

358
00:12:22,649 --> 00:12:25,976
Michael Goldston, finished a report on Area 2.

359
00:12:26,036 --> 00:12:26,815
His conclusion,

360
00:12:26,816 --> 00:12:29,376
in the department's own official language:

361
00:12:29,436 --> 00:12:31,709
the abuse was "systematic.

362
00:12:31,769 --> 00:12:33,774
It spanned more than a decade.

363
00:12:33,834 --> 00:12:35,733
It "included psychological techniques

364
00:12:35,734 --> 00:12:37,232
and planned torture.

365
00:12:37,292 --> 00:12:38,852
Planned torture —

366
00:12:38,912 --> 00:12:40,631
two words, typed by the police department,

367
00:12:40,632 --> 00:12:42,501
about the police department.

368
00:12:42,561 --> 00:12:44,440
The city's response was to lock the report

369
00:12:44,441 --> 00:12:45,820
in a drawer and fight for two years

370
00:12:45,821 --> 00:12:46,600
to keep it there,

371
00:12:46,601 --> 00:12:50,928
until a federal judge ordered it released in 1992.

372
00:12:50,988 --> 00:12:53,427
After that, something finally had to be done —

373
00:12:53,428 --> 00:12:56,615
and watch carefully what the something was.

374
00:12:56,675 --> 00:12:59,914
In February of 1993, after a disciplinary trial

375
00:12:59,915 --> 00:13:03,370
before the Police Board, Jon Burge was fired.

376
00:13:03,430 --> 00:13:04,552
Fired.

377
00:13:04,612 --> 00:13:07,092
For the physical abuse of Andrew Wilson —

378
00:13:07,152 --> 00:13:08,707
the board's own words.

379
00:13:08,767 --> 00:13:10,686
The two detectives charged alongside him

380
00:13:10,687 --> 00:13:13,187
were suspended and went back to work.

381
00:13:13,247 --> 00:13:15,527
And Burge packed up, left Chicago,

382
00:13:15,587 --> 00:13:17,626
and drove south to a house in Apollo Beach,

383
00:13:17,627 --> 00:13:20,639
Florida, near Tampa, where the water is warm.

384
00:13:20,699 --> 00:13:22,278
In time he had a police pension

385
00:13:22,279 --> 00:13:24,998
of roughly three thousand dollars a month,

386
00:13:24,999 --> 00:13:26,617
and a forty-foot cabin cruiser.

387
00:13:26,618 --> 00:13:28,716
He named the boat Vigilante.

388
00:13:28,776 --> 00:13:30,276
Under oath, years later,

389
00:13:30,336 --> 00:13:32,744
he would claim he picked the name at random.

390
00:13:32,745 --> 00:13:35,679
Count what the city knew by 1993.

391
00:13:35,739 --> 00:13:37,186
A doctor's letter.

392
00:13:37,246 --> 00:13:39,111
A supreme court reversal.

393
00:13:39,171 --> 00:13:40,944
A federal civil verdict.

394
00:13:41,004 --> 00:13:42,544
An inside informant.

395
00:13:42,604 --> 00:13:45,166
Twenty-three articles' worth of journalism.

396
00:13:45,226 --> 00:13:48,531
Its own report, with the words "planned torture.

397
00:13:48,591 --> 00:13:51,246
More than fifty named victims and counting.

398
00:13:51,306 --> 00:13:54,446
And the sum total of consequence for Jon Burge,

399
00:13:54,506 --> 00:13:57,226
commander of it all, was this:

400
00:13:57,286 --> 00:13:59,116
he lost his job.

401
00:13:59,176 --> 00:14:00,531
No handcuffs.

402
00:14:00,591 --> 00:14:01,946
No charges.

403
00:14:02,006 --> 00:14:03,128
No trial.

404
00:14:03,188 --> 00:14:04,607
A man was fired for torture,

405
00:14:04,608 --> 00:14:06,927
the way you might be fired for padding

406
00:14:06,928 --> 00:14:08,748
an expense report —

407
00:14:08,808 --> 00:14:11,514
and that, everyone assumed, was the end of it.

408
00:14:11,522 --> 00:14:12,877
It should have been.

409
00:14:12,937 --> 00:14:15,096
Because standing between Jon Burge

410
00:14:15,097 --> 00:14:17,156
and any courtroom was something stronger

411
00:14:17,157 --> 00:14:20,537
than money, stronger than the code of silence,

412
00:14:20,597 --> 00:14:22,558
stronger than City Hall.

413
00:14:22,618 --> 00:14:25,041
Something built into the law itself.

414
00:14:25,101 --> 00:14:28,513
And it had already run out.

415
00:14:28,573 --> 00:14:30,233
Every crime in Illinois —

416
00:14:30,293 --> 00:14:32,373
almost every crime anywhere —

417
00:14:32,433 --> 00:14:33,875
comes with a clock.

418
00:14:33,935 --> 00:14:36,034
It's called the statute of limitations,

419
00:14:36,035 --> 00:14:39,275
and the idea behind it is old and mostly decent:

420
00:14:39,335 --> 00:14:41,715
evidence rots, memories fade,

421
00:14:41,775 --> 00:14:44,034
and a just society shouldn't hang ancient

422
00:14:44,035 --> 00:14:46,435
accusations over a man forever.

423
00:14:46,495 --> 00:14:49,515
For the crimes that fit what happened at Area 2 —

424
00:14:49,575 --> 00:14:52,355
aggravated battery, official misconduct —

425
00:14:52,415 --> 00:14:55,559
Illinois law gave prosecutors three years.

426
00:14:55,619 --> 00:14:57,518
Three years from the moment the crank turned

427
00:14:57,519 --> 00:15:00,039
to the last day charges could be filed.

428
00:15:00,099 --> 00:15:01,918
Now run the arithmetic of this story

429
00:15:01,919 --> 00:15:03,404
against that clock.

430
00:15:03,464 --> 00:15:05,484
Anthony Holmes, 1973:

431
00:15:05,544 --> 00:15:07,930
the clock died in 1976.

432
00:15:07,990 --> 00:15:11,230
Andrew Wilson, February 1982:

433
00:15:11,290 --> 00:15:14,090
the clock died in February 1985 —

434
00:15:14,150 --> 00:15:16,489
while the letter about him sat unanswered

435
00:15:16,490 --> 00:15:18,958
in a prosecutor's filing system.

436
00:15:19,018 --> 00:15:20,597
Every victim of the eighties:

437
00:15:20,598 --> 00:15:22,677
expired by the early nineties,

438
00:15:22,678 --> 00:15:25,574
before the Goldston report ever saw daylight.

439
00:15:25,634 --> 00:15:27,407
Do you see the shape of it?

440
00:15:27,467 --> 00:15:30,493
The perfect crime is not the one nobody discovers.

441
00:15:30,553 --> 00:15:32,572
It's the one nobody with power believes

442
00:15:32,573 --> 00:15:35,313
until belief has an expiration date —

443
00:15:35,373 --> 00:15:37,912
and the men who ran Chicago managed not to believe

444
00:15:37,913 --> 00:15:40,081
this one for exactly long enough.

445
00:15:40,141 --> 00:15:42,796
The clock was never going to catch Jon Burge.

446
00:15:42,856 --> 00:15:44,416
The clock was his alibi,

447
00:15:44,476 --> 00:15:46,858
and the city's silence had wound it.

448
00:15:46,918 --> 00:15:49,077
But the victims would not stay quiet,

449
00:15:49,078 --> 00:15:50,717
and by the early 2000s

450
00:15:50,718 --> 00:15:53,247
the pressure had grown too great to ignore.

451
00:15:53,255 --> 00:15:54,455
In 2002,

452
00:15:54,515 --> 00:15:57,115
a judge appointed two special prosecutors —

453
00:15:57,175 --> 00:15:58,995
Edward Egan and Robert Boyle —

454
00:15:59,055 --> 00:16:00,655
with one assignment:

455
00:16:00,715 --> 00:16:03,295
investigate Area 2, all of it,

456
00:16:03,355 --> 00:16:04,974
and determine once and for all

457
00:16:04,975 --> 00:16:06,929
whether crimes had been committed.

458
00:16:06,930 --> 00:16:08,610
They worked for four years.

459
00:16:08,670 --> 00:16:11,046
They spent about seven million dollars.

460
00:16:11,106 --> 00:16:13,947
They examined one hundred and forty-eight claims

461
00:16:13,948 --> 00:16:14,470
of torture.

462
00:16:14,471 --> 00:16:17,050
And in July of 2006 they delivered a report

463
00:16:17,051 --> 00:16:19,031
that said, in substance,

464
00:16:19,091 --> 00:16:21,991
everything the victims had been saying
for thirty years:

465
00:16:22,051 --> 00:16:23,627
the abuse was real.

466
00:16:23,687 --> 00:16:25,946
They believed torture had occurred in roughly half

467
00:16:25,947 --> 00:16:27,828
the cases they examined.

468
00:16:27,888 --> 00:16:28,948
In three of them —

469
00:16:29,008 --> 00:16:30,708
Andrew Wilson's among them —

470
00:16:30,768 --> 00:16:33,488
they found proof beyond a reasonable doubt:

471
00:16:33,548 --> 00:16:34,907
enough evidence, right now,

472
00:16:34,908 --> 00:16:37,947
to convict Jon Burge and his men in any courtroom

473
00:16:37,948 --> 00:16:39,181
in America.

474
00:16:39,241 --> 00:16:40,735
Sit with that sentence.

475
00:16:40,795 --> 00:16:42,615
And then came the conclusion,

476
00:16:42,675 --> 00:16:44,914
the one the lawyers had known was coming

477
00:16:44,915 --> 00:16:47,394
and the survivors could hardly bear to hear:

478
00:16:47,395 --> 00:16:48,735
charges filed —

479
00:16:48,795 --> 00:16:49,719
none.

480
00:16:49,779 --> 00:16:51,878
Every provable crime was decades past

481
00:16:51,879 --> 00:16:53,456
its three-year clock.

482
00:16:53,516 --> 00:16:55,915
The special prosecutors had built a perfect case

483
00:16:55,916 --> 00:16:59,004
for a trial that could never legally begin.

484
00:16:59,064 --> 00:17:01,323
The state of Illinois had finally, officially,

485
00:17:01,324 --> 00:17:03,384
expensively believed the victims —

486
00:17:03,444 --> 00:17:05,852
and its belief was worth nothing.

487
00:17:05,912 --> 00:17:06,732
Except —

488
00:17:06,792 --> 00:17:08,652
and here the story turns —

489
00:17:08,712 --> 00:17:10,811
the confessions themselves were still doing

490
00:17:10,812 --> 00:17:13,954
damage, live, in the present tense.

491
00:17:14,014 --> 00:17:15,333
Because while the torturers

492
00:17:15,334 --> 00:17:17,154
could not be prosecuted,

493
00:17:17,214 --> 00:17:19,316
the tortured were still in prison.

494
00:17:19,376 --> 00:17:21,788
Some of them were waiting to be executed.

495
00:17:21,812 --> 00:17:24,632
They called themselves the Death Row Ten:

496
00:17:24,692 --> 00:17:26,171
condemned men in Illinois

497
00:17:26,172 --> 00:17:28,631
whose death sentences rested on confessions

498
00:17:28,632 --> 00:17:30,968
that had come out of Area 2.

499
00:17:31,028 --> 00:17:32,687
Through the late nineties they organized

500
00:17:32,688 --> 00:17:34,408
from inside their cells —

501
00:17:34,468 --> 00:17:36,328
leaflets typed on death row,

502
00:17:36,388 --> 00:17:38,148
mothers marching outside,

503
00:17:38,208 --> 00:17:40,290
lawyers digging through transcripts.

504
00:17:40,291 --> 00:17:41,830
Their cases landed on the desk

505
00:17:41,831 --> 00:17:43,611
of Governor George Ryan,

506
00:17:43,671 --> 00:17:45,950
a Republican pharmacist from small-town Illinois

507
00:17:45,951 --> 00:17:47,190
who had supported

508
00:17:47,191 --> 00:17:48,890
the death penalty his entire career,

509
00:17:48,891 --> 00:17:50,930
and who now found himself reading,

510
00:17:50,931 --> 00:17:52,130
case after case,

511
00:17:52,131 --> 00:17:55,113
about the box and the bag and the radiator.

512
00:17:55,173 --> 00:17:58,803
In 2000, he froze every execution in the state.

513
00:17:58,863 --> 00:18:02,503
And in January 2003, in his final days in office,

514
00:18:02,563 --> 00:18:05,122
he did two things no American governor had done

515
00:18:05,123 --> 00:18:06,441
in living memory.

516
00:18:06,501 --> 00:18:07,701
On January 10th,

517
00:18:07,761 --> 00:18:10,400
he pardoned four of the Death Row Ten outright —

518
00:18:10,401 --> 00:18:13,921
Aaron Patterson, Madison Hobley, Leroy Orange,

519
00:18:13,981 --> 00:18:15,161
Stanley Howard —

520
00:18:15,221 --> 00:18:17,421
pardoned on the grounds of innocence:

521
00:18:17,481 --> 00:18:19,360
men tortured into confessing crimes

522
00:18:19,361 --> 00:18:22,113
the state now said they did not commit.

523
00:18:22,173 --> 00:18:24,672
Four men walked off death row not through some

524
00:18:24,673 --> 00:18:26,832
technicality but because the governor

525
00:18:26,833 --> 00:18:28,492
of Illinois declared the confessions

526
00:18:28,493 --> 00:18:31,515
that condemned them to be the fruit of torture.

527
00:18:31,575 --> 00:18:34,555
The next day he emptied death row entirely —

528
00:18:34,615 --> 00:18:37,914
one hundred and sixty-seven commutations —

529
00:18:37,915 --> 00:18:39,994
telling a law-school audience:

530
00:18:39,995 --> 00:18:42,755
"Our capital system is haunted by the demon

531
00:18:42,815 --> 00:18:44,715
of error:

532
00:18:44,775 --> 00:18:47,941
error in determining guilt,
and error in determining who among

533
00:18:47,942 --> 00:18:48,499
the guilty deserves to die."

534
00:18:48,500 --> 00:18:50,219
Hold the split screen for a moment,

535
00:18:50,220 --> 00:18:52,873
because the whole scandal lives inside it.

536
00:18:52,933 --> 00:18:53,733
In Chicago,

537
00:18:53,773 --> 00:18:55,652
tortured men are walking out of prison,

538
00:18:55,653 --> 00:18:59,853
blinking in the cold, pardoned, apologized to,

539
00:18:59,913 --> 00:19:02,368
their confessions declared poison.

540
00:19:02,428 --> 00:19:03,847
And eleven hundred miles south,

541
00:19:03,848 --> 00:19:05,828
in the Florida sunshine,

542
00:19:05,888 --> 00:19:07,447
the man whose detectives extracted

543
00:19:07,448 --> 00:19:09,147
those confessions is stepping

544
00:19:09,148 --> 00:19:11,448
onto a boat called Vigilante,

545
00:19:11,508 --> 00:19:13,768
his pension arriving on schedule,

546
00:19:13,828 --> 00:19:15,468
legally untouchable —

547
00:19:15,528 --> 00:19:17,407
a man officially confirmed as the author

548
00:19:17,408 --> 00:19:19,688
of systematic, planned torture,

549
00:19:19,748 --> 00:19:21,187
and officially beyond the reach

550
00:19:21,188 --> 00:19:23,520
of any criminal court on earth for it.

551
00:19:23,580 --> 00:19:25,551
That should be the end of the story.

552
00:19:25,552 --> 00:19:26,191
It is not —

553
00:19:26,192 --> 00:19:27,991
because of four sheets of paper,

554
00:19:27,992 --> 00:19:30,761
and a man who could not stop lying.

555
00:19:30,821 --> 00:19:34,160
In November 2003, with Ryan's pardons still fresh

556
00:19:34,161 --> 00:19:38,261
in the city's memory, lawyers for Madison Hobley —

557
00:19:38,321 --> 00:19:39,580
one of the four pardoned men,

558
00:19:39,581 --> 00:19:42,421
now suing Burge in federal court —

559
00:19:42,481 --> 00:19:44,840
sent Burge a routine set of written questions,

560
00:19:44,841 --> 00:19:47,036
called interrogatories.

561
00:19:47,096 --> 00:19:49,036
Standard civil procedure;

562
00:19:49,096 --> 00:19:51,331
lawyers exchange them by the thousand.

563
00:19:51,343 --> 00:19:53,562
Did you ever use torture or abuse on suspects

564
00:19:53,563 --> 00:19:54,787
at Area 2?

565
00:19:54,847 --> 00:19:56,620
Were you ever aware of it?

566
00:19:56,680 --> 00:19:58,719
Burge could have refused to answer —

567
00:19:58,720 --> 00:20:01,703
pleaded the Fifth, as other Area 2 men did.

568
00:20:01,763 --> 00:20:03,402
Instead, from Florida, he wrote back:

569
00:20:03,403 --> 00:20:04,279
no.

570
00:20:04,339 --> 00:20:05,601
Never used it.

571
00:20:05,661 --> 00:20:06,969
Never saw it.

572
00:20:07,029 --> 00:20:08,337
Never knew of it.

573
00:20:08,397 --> 00:20:10,773
And he signed his name under oath.

574
00:20:10,833 --> 00:20:12,931
He thought he was swatting a fly.

575
00:20:12,991 --> 00:20:14,350
He had just handed the government

576
00:20:14,351 --> 00:20:18,168
the only weapon it would ever get.

577
00:20:18,228 --> 00:20:20,929
Here is the move, and it is almost elegant.

578
00:20:20,989 --> 00:20:24,829
The torture of 1973, of 1982, of 1991 —

579
00:20:24,889 --> 00:20:26,969
legally dead, every count of it,

580
00:20:27,029 --> 00:20:29,449
sealed behind the statute of limitations.

581
00:20:29,509 --> 00:20:30,348
But a lie signed

582
00:20:30,349 --> 00:20:33,882
in November 2003 is not a crime of 1982.

583
00:20:33,942 --> 00:20:36,422
It is a brand-new federal felony —

584
00:20:36,482 --> 00:20:38,782
perjury, obstruction of justice —

585
00:20:38,842 --> 00:20:40,661
committed the moment the pen left the paper,

586
00:20:40,662 --> 00:20:43,331
with a clock that starts fresh.

587
00:20:43,391 --> 00:20:45,490
The federal government could never punish Jon

588
00:20:45,491 --> 00:20:47,068
Burge for what he did.

589
00:20:47,128 --> 00:20:48,487
But when he denied under oath

590
00:20:48,488 --> 00:20:49,788
that he had done it,

591
00:20:49,848 --> 00:20:52,755
the denial itself was a crime it could punish.

592
00:20:52,815 --> 00:20:54,534
The United States Attorney in Chicago

593
00:20:54,535 --> 00:20:56,274
at the time was Patrick Fitzgerald,

594
00:20:56,275 --> 00:20:58,953
and his office saw the opening.

595
00:20:59,013 --> 00:21:01,793
As he put it, with a prosecutor's bluntness,

596
00:21:01,853 --> 00:21:03,172
the statutes of limitations

597
00:21:03,173 --> 00:21:05,104
on the old crimes were gone.

598
00:21:05,164 --> 00:21:06,658
The lie was not.

599
00:21:06,718 --> 00:21:09,738
On the morning of October 21st, 2008,

600
00:21:09,798 --> 00:21:12,577
FBI agents walked up the drive of a tidy house

601
00:21:12,578 --> 00:21:16,438
in Apollo Beach, Florida, and arrested Jon Burge —

602
00:21:16,498 --> 00:21:18,998
twenty-six years after Dr. Raba's letter,

603
00:21:19,058 --> 00:21:21,137
fifteen years after the firing that was supposed

604
00:21:21,138 --> 00:21:22,608
to be the end of it.

605
00:21:22,668 --> 00:21:24,128
The charge sheet read:

606
00:21:24,188 --> 00:21:26,147
two counts of obstruction of justice,

607
00:21:26,148 --> 00:21:27,598
one count of perjury.

608
00:21:27,658 --> 00:21:28,757
Announcing the indictment,

609
00:21:28,758 --> 00:21:31,377
Fitzgerald said something worth engraving:

610
00:21:31,378 --> 00:21:33,897
"There is no place for torture and abuse

611
00:21:33,898 --> 00:21:35,375
in a police station.

612
00:21:35,435 --> 00:21:37,994
There is no place for perjury and false statements

613
00:21:37,995 --> 00:21:39,669
in federal lawsuits."

614
00:21:39,729 --> 00:21:41,788
The trial, in the spring of 2010,

615
00:21:41,789 --> 00:21:43,788
was the trial Chicago had dodged

616
00:21:43,789 --> 00:21:45,349
for a generation —

617
00:21:45,409 --> 00:21:46,880
because to prove the lie,

618
00:21:46,909 --> 00:21:49,223
the government had to prove the torture.

619
00:21:49,224 --> 00:21:50,244
For five weeks,

620
00:21:50,304 --> 00:21:52,663
a federal courtroom heard everything the statute

621
00:21:52,664 --> 00:21:54,665
of limitations had buried.

622
00:21:54,725 --> 00:21:55,645
Anthony Holmes,

623
00:21:55,705 --> 00:21:58,124
thirty-seven years after his night with the box,

624
00:21:58,125 --> 00:22:00,325
finally testifying to a jury:

625
00:22:00,385 --> 00:22:03,151
"He plugged it into the wall and came towards me.

626
00:22:03,152 --> 00:22:04,553
Melvin Jones.

627
00:22:04,613 --> 00:22:06,107
The medical records.

628
00:22:06,167 --> 00:22:07,289
The letter.

629
00:22:07,349 --> 00:22:09,828
The defense answered the only way it could —

630
00:22:09,829 --> 00:22:13,029
these men are convicts, liars, cop-haters —

631
00:22:13,089 --> 00:22:14,548
the same argument that had worked

632
00:22:14,549 --> 00:22:15,995
for thirty years.

633
00:22:16,055 --> 00:22:17,828
It did not work this time.

634
00:22:17,888 --> 00:22:19,848
On June 28th, 2010,

635
00:22:19,908 --> 00:22:22,772
the jury found Jon Burge guilty on every count.

636
00:22:22,832 --> 00:22:24,951
In the corridor outside, Melvin Jones,

637
00:22:24,952 --> 00:22:27,611
tortured nine days before Andrew Wilson, said:

638
00:22:27,612 --> 00:22:29,252
"I got justice,

639
00:22:29,312 --> 00:22:31,900
something I have been wanting for 28 years."

640
00:22:31,956 --> 00:22:34,856
At sentencing, in January 2011,

641
00:22:34,916 --> 00:22:36,151
Burge told the court:

642
00:22:36,196 --> 00:22:37,976
"I am 63 years old,

643
00:22:38,036 --> 00:22:40,276
and while I try to keep a proud face,

644
00:22:40,336 --> 00:22:42,645
in reality I am a broken man.

645
00:22:42,705 --> 00:22:43,684
Anthony Holmes rose

646
00:22:43,685 --> 00:22:46,124
and answered him without raising his voice:

647
00:22:46,125 --> 00:22:47,496
"I don't hate him.

648
00:22:47,556 --> 00:22:49,027
I hate what he did to me,

649
00:22:49,056 --> 00:22:51,095
and I hate the fact that nobody would listen

650
00:22:51,096 --> 00:22:53,315
to me when I tried to tell people about it;

651
00:22:53,316 --> 00:22:54,808
it fell on deaf ears.

652
00:22:54,868 --> 00:22:57,067
Judge Joan Lefkow asked the question that hangs

653
00:22:57,068 --> 00:22:58,928
over this whole story —

654
00:22:58,988 --> 00:23:00,947
"How can one trust the system of justice

655
00:23:00,948 --> 00:23:03,728
when the system is so defiled?" —

656
00:23:03,788 --> 00:23:06,147
and sentenced Jon Burge to four and a half years

657
00:23:06,148 --> 00:23:07,600
in federal prison.

658
00:23:07,660 --> 00:23:09,340
Four and a half years.

659
00:23:09,400 --> 00:23:10,619
I am not going to dress

660
00:23:10,620 --> 00:23:12,380
that number up for you.

661
00:23:12,440 --> 00:23:14,640
More than a hundred men tortured;

662
00:23:14,700 --> 00:23:16,540
men who served ten, twenty,

663
00:23:16,600 --> 00:23:20,060
thirty years on confessions cranked out of a box;

664
00:23:20,120 --> 00:23:23,060
two decades of a city's willful deafness —

665
00:23:23,120 --> 00:23:25,039
and the bill, when it finally came,

666
00:23:25,040 --> 00:23:27,699
was fifty-four months, for lying about it,

667
00:23:27,700 --> 00:23:30,199
served mostly in a low-security federal facility

668
00:23:30,200 --> 00:23:31,581
in North Carolina.

669
00:23:31,641 --> 00:23:33,440
If that number feels like an insult,

670
00:23:33,441 --> 00:23:34,641
trust the feeling;

671
00:23:34,701 --> 00:23:35,840
it is a fraction of what

672
00:23:35,841 --> 00:23:38,061
the underlying conduct deserved,

673
00:23:38,121 --> 00:23:40,120
the going rate of a system that had let

674
00:23:40,121 --> 00:23:41,958
the real charges die.

675
00:23:42,018 --> 00:23:42,878
And yet —

676
00:23:42,938 --> 00:23:44,478
hold both truths —

677
00:23:44,538 --> 00:23:45,497
it was more than any

678
00:23:45,498 --> 00:23:47,784
of his victims had ever been promised.

679
00:23:47,844 --> 00:23:49,823
In the entire history of the scandal,

680
00:23:49,824 --> 00:23:52,263
it is the only criminal sentence anyone ever

681
00:23:52,264 --> 00:23:54,632
served for what happened at Area 2.

682
00:23:54,692 --> 00:23:56,411
The man who beat the clock was beaten,

683
00:23:56,412 --> 00:23:58,926
in the end, by his own signature.

684
00:23:58,986 --> 00:24:00,104
Chicago, meanwhile,

685
00:24:00,126 --> 00:24:02,536
was not finished insulting the survivors.

686
00:24:02,537 --> 00:24:04,077
Days after the sentencing,

687
00:24:04,137 --> 00:24:05,976
the police pension board met to decide

688
00:24:05,977 --> 00:24:07,516
whether a man imprisoned for lying

689
00:24:07,517 --> 00:24:10,276
about torture should keep collecting his police

690
00:24:10,277 --> 00:24:11,368
pension.

691
00:24:11,428 --> 00:24:13,944
Four civilian members voted to strip it.

692
00:24:14,004 --> 00:24:16,659
Four police members voted to let him keep it.

693
00:24:16,719 --> 00:24:18,039
Four to four —

694
00:24:18,099 --> 00:24:19,079
a tie —

695
00:24:19,139 --> 00:24:21,882
and under the rules, a tie meant Burge won.

696
00:24:21,942 --> 00:24:24,001
The state's attorney general fought it all the way

697
00:24:24,002 --> 00:24:26,733
to the Illinois Supreme Court and lost.

698
00:24:26,793 --> 00:24:28,632
Every month, from his cell and then

699
00:24:28,633 --> 00:24:31,793
from his Florida living room, the pension arrived:

700
00:24:31,853 --> 00:24:34,492
the city paying the man its own report had named,

701
00:24:34,493 --> 00:24:37,652
roughly three thousand dollars at a time,

702
00:24:37,653 --> 00:24:39,154
until the day he died.

703
00:24:39,214 --> 00:24:41,994
He walked out of custody in February 2015,

704
00:24:42,054 --> 00:24:44,841
unbroken in the only way that mattered to him.

705
00:24:44,901 --> 00:24:46,520
Within weeks, when word came

706
00:24:46,521 --> 00:24:48,720
that Chicago was considering paying reparations

707
00:24:48,721 --> 00:24:50,161
to his victims,

708
00:24:50,221 --> 00:24:52,909
Burge released a statement through a police blog.

709
00:24:52,910 --> 00:24:57,309
He wrote that he found it hard to believe
the city's leadership "could even contemplate

710
00:24:57,310 --> 00:24:59,837
giving 'Reparations' to human vermin.

711
00:24:59,897 --> 00:25:01,344
Human vermin.

712
00:25:01,404 --> 00:25:03,687
Keep those two words in your ear.

713
00:25:03,747 --> 00:25:05,546
They are the last public words that matter

714
00:25:05,547 --> 00:25:06,587
from Jon Burge,

715
00:25:06,647 --> 00:25:09,235
and the city was about to answer them.

716
00:25:09,295 --> 00:25:11,255
On May 6th, 2015,

717
00:25:11,315 --> 00:25:13,954
the Chicago City Council did something no city

718
00:25:13,955 --> 00:25:16,754
in the history of the United States had ever done:

719
00:25:16,755 --> 00:25:20,674
it passed reparations —

720
00:25:20,675 --> 00:25:22,328
formal, legislated reparations —

721
00:25:22,329 --> 00:25:25,393
for police violence.
Not a court settlement forced by lawyers.

722
00:25:25,453 --> 00:25:26,912
A law, passed unanimously,

723
00:25:26,913 --> 00:25:29,130
in the name of the city itself.

724
00:25:29,190 --> 00:25:32,409
A five-and-a-half-million-dollar fund
for fifty-seven living survivors of Area 2

725
00:25:32,410 --> 00:25:35,374
and Area 3.

726
00:25:35,434 --> 00:25:39,034
A formal apology, delivered on the council floor —

727
00:25:39,094 --> 00:25:41,213
the mayor calling the torture "a stain

728
00:25:41,214 --> 00:25:43,553
on the reputation of this great city"

729
00:25:43,554 --> 00:25:46,654
as survivors watched from the gallery,

730
00:25:46,714 --> 00:25:48,493
men who had waited thirty years to hear

731
00:25:48,494 --> 00:25:51,046
an official mouth say the word sorry.

732
00:25:51,106 --> 00:25:53,826
Free tuition at the city's colleges —

733
00:25:53,886 --> 00:25:55,105
not just for survivors,

734
00:25:55,106 --> 00:25:57,445
but for their children and their grandchildren,

735
00:25:57,446 --> 00:25:59,885
because torture runs down generations

736
00:25:59,886 --> 00:26:02,213
and the city finally admitted it.

737
00:26:02,273 --> 00:26:04,833
A counseling center on the South Side,

738
00:26:04,893 --> 00:26:07,393
the Chicago Torture Justice Center,

739
00:26:07,453 --> 00:26:09,492
built specifically to treat the survivors

740
00:26:09,493 --> 00:26:11,337
of its own police force.

741
00:26:11,397 --> 00:26:12,917
And one more thing —

742
00:26:12,977 --> 00:26:15,116
the one Jon Burge could never buy back

743
00:26:15,117 --> 00:26:17,814
with a pension or outrun on a boat.

744
00:26:17,874 --> 00:26:20,761
The ordinance ordered the story into the schools.

745
00:26:20,821 --> 00:26:24,461
Since 2018, every Chicago public-school student —

746
00:26:24,521 --> 00:26:26,941
eighth grade, and again in tenth —

747
00:26:27,001 --> 00:26:28,940
is taught a required history unit

748
00:26:28,941 --> 00:26:31,324
about what happened at Area 2.

749
00:26:31,384 --> 00:26:32,384
It has a name:

750
00:26:32,444 --> 00:26:34,085
"Reparations Won.

751
00:26:34,145 --> 00:26:38,285
The tally of victims, the box, the buried letter,

752
00:26:38,345 --> 00:26:39,785
the men who fought —

753
00:26:39,845 --> 00:26:42,558
printed, bound, mandatory.

754
00:26:42,618 --> 00:26:43,518
Anthony Holmes,

755
00:26:43,578 --> 00:26:46,037
who once said his words fell on deaf ears,

756
00:26:46,038 --> 00:26:48,722
has told his story to the teachers who teach it.

757
00:26:48,723 --> 00:26:51,146
The children of Chicago are the ears now.

758
00:26:51,206 --> 00:26:53,745
Jon Burge died on September 19th, 2018,

759
00:26:53,746 --> 00:26:56,166
in Florida, at seventy —

760
00:26:56,226 --> 00:26:59,086
pension intact, denials intact,

761
00:26:59,146 --> 00:27:01,291
unrepentant to the last breath.

762
00:27:01,351 --> 00:27:04,006
And the truth simply kept going without him.

763
00:27:04,066 --> 00:27:06,825
Courts have gone on overturning Area 2 convictions

764
00:27:06,826 --> 00:27:09,666
after his death, year after year —

765
00:27:09,726 --> 00:27:11,746
Jackie Wilson, Andrew's brother,

766
00:27:11,806 --> 00:27:15,545
declared innocent in 2020, and more men in 2024,

767
00:27:15,546 --> 00:27:19,085
in 2025, with hundreds of claims still moving

768
00:27:19,086 --> 00:27:21,125
through a state torture commission that exists,

769
00:27:21,126 --> 00:27:24,414
uniquely in America, because of this one man.

770
00:27:24,474 --> 00:27:28,821
The public bill passed two hundred
and ten million dollars years ago

771
00:27:28,822 --> 00:27:31,291
and is still climbing.
And in the summer of 2026,

772
00:27:31,292 --> 00:27:33,432
on Martin Luther King Drive,

773
00:27:33,492 --> 00:27:34,751
the city finally broke ground

774
00:27:34,752 --> 00:27:37,652
on a permanent memorial to the survivors —

775
00:27:37,712 --> 00:27:40,531
eleven years late, like everything in this story,

776
00:27:40,532 --> 00:27:43,186
but coming.

777
00:27:43,246 --> 00:27:44,833
So go back to the letter.

778
00:27:44,893 --> 00:27:46,733
February 1982:

779
00:27:46,793 --> 00:27:50,753
one page, one doctor doing his job, three words —

780
00:27:50,813 --> 00:27:52,749
a thorough investigation.

781
00:27:52,809 --> 00:27:55,449
It was the truth, complete and sufficient,

782
00:27:55,509 --> 00:27:56,996
thirty-three years early.

783
00:27:57,056 --> 00:27:58,375
Everything the city ever needed

784
00:27:58,376 --> 00:27:59,655
to know was on that page,

785
00:27:59,656 --> 00:28:01,295
and the page was buried,

786
00:28:01,296 --> 00:28:04,616
and the burying cost a hundred men their bodies

787
00:28:04,676 --> 00:28:07,975
and their years, and taxpayers a fortune,

788
00:28:07,976 --> 00:28:09,754
and two more decades of poisoned verdicts.

789
00:28:09,755 --> 00:28:11,873
Now look at where the page ended up.

790
00:28:11,913 --> 00:28:14,172
The man who buried the truth beat every charge

791
00:28:14,173 --> 00:28:15,493
the law could name —

792
00:28:15,553 --> 00:28:18,190
except the one he committed by denying it.

793
00:28:18,250 --> 00:28:19,883
He kept his pension.

794
00:28:19,943 --> 00:28:21,344
He kept his boat.

795
00:28:21,404 --> 00:28:22,363
He kept his contempt,

796
00:28:22,364 --> 00:28:24,523
all the way to "human vermin.

797
00:28:24,583 --> 00:28:26,582
And he still lost, more completely

798
00:28:26,583 --> 00:28:29,407
than a prison sentence could ever have managed —

799
00:28:29,423 --> 00:28:31,462
because the thing he spent forty years calling

800
00:28:31,463 --> 00:28:34,123
a lie is now taught, by law,

801
00:28:34,183 --> 00:28:36,201
to every child in Chicago.

802
00:28:36,261 --> 00:28:38,637
His victims' names are in the textbook.

803
00:28:38,697 --> 00:28:39,877
His is too —

804
00:28:39,937 --> 00:28:41,491
as the man who did it.

805
00:28:41,551 --> 00:28:44,113
He beat the statute of limitations.

806
00:28:44,173 --> 00:28:45,992
Nobody beats the curriculum.

807
00:28:46,052 --> 00:28:48,939
The letter was buried in a file in 1982.

808
00:28:48,999 --> 00:28:51,004
It came back as a chapter.

809
00:28:51,064 --> 00:28:52,883
That is the whole moral of this story,

810
00:28:52,884 --> 00:28:55,204
and it is worth carrying with you:

811
00:28:55,264 --> 00:28:57,704
the truth can be ignored, outwaited,

812
00:28:57,764 --> 00:28:58,803
even legally forgiven —

813
00:28:58,804 --> 00:29:00,763
and still refuse to die,

814
00:29:00,764 --> 00:29:03,471
as long as somebody keeps writing it down.

815
00:29:03,531 --> 00:29:06,311
If you think the letter should have been enough
the first time —

816
00:29:06,371 --> 00:29:09,131
hit like, so the next one gets read.
